<service>
  <id>laguna-alloy</id>
  <name>Laguna Escondida - Telemetry sidecar (Grafana Alloy)</name>
  <description>Receives the edge node's OTLP logs and traces on 127.0.0.1:4317/:4318, scrapes its Prometheus metrics on 127.0.0.1:9090, buffers to disk within a hard cap while the box is offline, and forwards everything to the cloud telemetry ingest. Holds no Grafana Cloud credentials.</description>

  <executable>%BASE%\..\artifacts\alloy\alloy.exe</executable>

  <!--
    The stability.level flag is REQUIRED, not optional: the disk buffer
    (otelcol.storage.file in alloy\config.alloy) is a public-preview component and
    Alloy refuses to start without it. Dropping that flag turns the offline buffer
    into a startup crash-loop.

    storage.path is Alloy's OWN component state, separate from the telemetry
    buffer; the buffer lives under LAGUNA_TELEMETRY_BUFFER_DIR.

    disable-reporting stops the anonymous usage ping to Grafana: pointless on a box
    that is offline half the time, and one less outbound call to explain.
  -->
  <arguments>run --stability.level=public-preview --disable-reporting --server.http.listen-addr=127.0.0.1:12345 --storage.path="%BASE%\..\alloy-data\wal" "%BASE%\..\alloy\config.alloy"</arguments>
  <workingdirectory>%BASE%\..\artifacts\alloy</workingdirectory>

  <!--
    DELIBERATELY no dependency, in either direction.

    Windows refuses to start a service whose dependency failed, so making
    laguna-edge-node depend on this one would let a broken telemetry sidecar keep
    the POS down. Telemetry must never be able to do that. The app tolerates the
    collector being absent (its OTLP exporter just fails in the background), and
    Alloy tolerates the app being absent (the scrape fails until it is back), so
    the two can start in any order.
  -->
  <startmode>Automatic</startmode>
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="10 sec"/>
  <stoptimeout>30 sec</stoptimeout>

  <!--
    All config comes from MACHINE env vars written by scripts\install.ps1 from
    env\alloy.env, so this committed XML holds no per-box values and no secrets.
    The node key is not an env var at all: Alloy reads it from the file named by
    LAGUNA_NODE_KEY_FILE.
  -->

  <logpath>%BASE%\..\logs</logpath>
  <!-- Alloy's own diagnostics only; the telemetry it forwards never lands here. -->
  <log mode="roll-by-size">
    <sizeThreshold>5120</sizeThreshold>
    <keepFiles>4</keepFiles>
  </log>
</service>
